# Maintainer: Velle Sinclair <brncomputerhelp@gmail.com>
pkgname=linux-wallpaperengine
pkgver=0.1.0
# 22: the same patches; prepare() changed, so an update can build them.
# makepkg reuses src/ between runs, and 15-21 each built once per checkout:
# the next run failed on a patch that had already added its file, and a
# build/ kept from an older release left stale objects behind (see
# prepare()). The bump makes a box that built one of them on such a tree
# build again from scratch.
#
# 21: 61 patches on the pinned commit, and the glslang one. 0054-0061 are
# SynapseOS's own.
#
#   - A lit image (genericimage2, 3 or 4 with LIGHTING) without effects
#     drew over a quarter of the scene (0054): such a shader places its
#     vertices with the model and view-projection matrices, which every
#     pass got in the image's own space. Its last pass gets the camera's.
#   - Media events carry what Wallpaper Engine's do (0055): a track's
#     length and position in seconds (MPRIS counts microseconds, so a
#     progress bar crawled and a duration read in the millions), and the
#     album artist, genres, subtitle, content type and text color a script
#     falls back on or colors with.
#   - Mat4, Mat3 and a layer's getTransformMatrix (0056), with Wallpaper
#     Engine's whole matrix API.
#   - A vector's x, y, z and w are its own properties (0057), so
#     value.hasOwnProperty ("x") tells a vector from a number.
#   - Scripts run in the scene's order (0058), not in the alphabetical
#     order of their property names, so one that reads what an earlier
#     layer's init put in `shared` finds it.
#   - A script sets a text's font and alignment (0059): a music widget
#     left-aligns its title when it mirrors it beside the album cover, and
#     a clock takes the font and alignment its settings pick.
#   - A color a script sets as a Vec3 keeps its alpha (0060). A text draws
#     its color's alpha, which the Vec3 left at 0, so every text a script
#     colored drew nothing: a music widget's title, artist and clock.
#   - The first frame's engine.frametime is 0 (0061), not the seconds spent
#     loading, which ended a one-second fade in its first frame.
#
# Measured as for 20 on the same 139 wallpapers. With 0053-0058, three
# wallpapers that drew a white screen or a quarter of their scene draw as
# their previews; one of them, now mostly black, is the one verdict that
# drops. The other two verdicts that change rise. The script errors two music
# widgets raised under a live media session are gone, and none are new.
# 0059-0061 change one verdict, on an animated wallpaper whose verdict also
# changed the other way between the two previous runs. Under a stand-in
# media player, the music widget's title draws in its panel, where it drew
# nowhere before.
#
# 20: 53 patches on the pinned commit, and the glslang one. 0051-0053 are
# SynapseOS's own.
#
#   - A text layer's effects draw (0052): 27 text layers in 10 wallpapers
#     carry them, mostly a blur that softens a clock, and also chromatic
#     aberration, VHS, film grain, water, shake and tint. The text draws, in
#     its color, onto a canvas of its own, and an image draws that canvas
#     through the layer's effects, as Wallpaper Engine draws it. It needed
#     0051 first: a file the engine adds to a scene read whole only the
#     first time it was opened.
#   - Music player widgets keep their scripts on a solid layer sized 0x0,
#     which drew at the scene's size and covered the wallpaper in white
#     (0053). It draws nothing now.
#
# Measured as for 19 on the same 139 wallpapers with 0051-0052: every
# verdict matches 18's but two, on wallpapers without text (one the rig
# caught early under 18, one an animation caught at another frame). The ten
# with text effects were compared with their previews. 0053 was measured on
# the four wallpapers with such a layer: two now draw as their previews, one
# draws its background on a quarter of the screen only (a fault of its own
# that the white hid), and one hides the layer and is unchanged.
#
# 19: 50 patches on the pinned commit, and the glslang one. 0047-0050 are
# SynapseOS's own, and make text layers work: clocks, dates, now-playing
# titles, widget labels.
#
#   - A string a property script returned was stored as `true` (0047): a
#     const char* picked DynamicValue's bool overload. A text whose script
#     returns a number (`date.getDate ()`) shows the number, as Wallpaper
#     Engine does.
#   - A text box's user setting reached scripts with its JSON quotes on
#     (0048). A widget wallpaper that places its panels from "1920/1980"
#     parsed every position as NaN and drew nothing at all.
#   - A text layer's script ran in a runtime of its own, without
#     applyUserProperties, `shared`, the media events or most of `engine`
#     (0049): 176 scripts in 22 wallpapers, and a date drew its month as
#     "undefined". It now runs like any other property script.
#   - Text broke no lines, read bytes instead of UTF-8, ignored its
#     alignment and angle, and took a depth from its z that put a clock and
#     a day name outside the camera (0050). `systemfont_consolas`, which
#     lays calendars out in columns, gets a monospaced face.
#
# A text layer still draws without its effects (a blurred glow behind a
# clock).
#
# Measured as for 18 on the same 139 wallpapers: every verdict is the same
# but the one whose first frame caught the rig early under 18, which drew
# from the first frame this time. The 23 wallpapers with text were compared
# frame by frame with 18: what differs is the text these patches fix, and
# their own clocks and animation.
#
# 18: 46 patches on the pinned commit, and the glslang one. 0046 is
# SynapseOS's own: a layer or an effect that a script shows or hides draws
# accordingly. A layer's passes were wired once, at load, so one hidden then
# had no pass drawing onto the scene and never appeared when a script showed
# it (49 layers in 7 wallpapers start hidden behind a script: settings
# panels, drag handles, controllers); an effect hidden at load was never
# built, and hiding a built one did nothing. The passes are wired again when
# the visibility changes, and only then. An effect hidden at load is built
# only when a script or a timeline drives its visibility.
#
# Measured as for 17 on the same 139 wallpapers: every verdict is the same
# but one wallpaper whose first frame caught the rig before the engine's
# surface was up; run three more times on each of 17 and 18, it drew from
# the first frame every time. The two other cases whose measures moved are
# the Matrix rain's particles, black on both, and a web page's audio
# visualizer. The wallpapers with scripted visibility were compared frame by
# frame and differ only by their own animation and clocks.
#
# 17: 45 patches on the pinned commit, and the glslang one. 0043-0045 are
# SynapseOS's own: scripts and keyframes run on effect and particle values,
# where 16 ran only those on a layer's own properties.
#
#   - An effect's material properties run their scripts and timeline
#     animations (0043): 62 scripts in 12 wallpapers and 4 keyframed values
#     in 3 sat at their static values, among them a media widget's play
#     button taking the accent colour and god rays following the music. A
#     script there sees the effect as thisObject, as Wallpaper Engine binds
#     it, so `thisObject.color = shared.accentColor` tints the effect and not
#     the whole layer.
#   - engine.userProperties exists (0044). A day/night scene reads its
#     "timeofday" setting through it, threw on every frame, and showed night
#     whatever the setting.
#   - A particle layer's instance overrides run their scripts and keyframes,
#     and the emitters read the emission rate and lifetime as they change
#     (0045): 264 scripts in 6 wallpapers, most of them an emission rate or a
#     dot size following the music.
#
# Measured as for 16 on the same 139 wallpapers, and every verdict is the
# same but one: a web wallpaper whose visualizer follows whatever the desktop
# is playing, which scores the same on 16 when the two are run together.
# Every case whose frame, log or image measures moved was looked at. Besides
# audio and video timing, one wallpaper logs a script error fewer, and the
# Matrix rain spawns a quarter of its particles in silence, as its own rate
# script sets (minvalue 0.25).
#
# 16: 42 patches on the pinned commit, and one on the glslang fork it builds as
# a submodule (glslang-0001, applied inside it). 0029-0042 and the glslang
# patch are SynapseOS's own; the note for 15 below still describes 0001-0028.
# Measured the same way on the same 139 wallpapers: the classifier passes the
# same 130 as for 15, because these fixes work inside wallpapers that already
# drew something. None got worse: every case's frame, log and image measures
# were compared with the run before this batch, and each one that moved was
# looked at.
#
#   - Scene scripts can use what they are written against. Vector methods
#     (a.add (b), mix, dot) were unreachable and ended every script that
#     called one (0030); once reachable they ran with their operands swapped
#     and a bad argument took the whole engine down (0034). A vector can be
#     built from a smaller one (0038). WEMath and WEColor exported nothing,
#     so 20 scripts in 8 wallpapers and 9 in 8 failed on their first call
#     (0037). Timers never ran (0041). Layers answer getTextureAnimation,
#     getParent, getChildren and name, the scene enumerateLayers, and the
#     engine its device checks (0031-0033).
#   - Every script's init () runs before any update (), and each is handed
#     the user's settings through applyUserProperties (), which 88 scripts in
#     12 wallpapers read theirs from and which was never called (0040).
#   - What a script sets on a layer's scale, angles or visible reaches the
#     layer (0035): it landed in a group value nothing reads, so an analog
#     clock's hands sat at 12:00, and 135 scale, 16 angles and 306 visible
#     scripts went nowhere.
#   - Timeline animations play (0039): 37 keyframed properties in 10
#     wallpapers, layers that fade in at load among them, sat still. A hidden
#     group hides its layers (0036), so a wallpaper with a group per language
#     shows one language instead of all of them stacked.
#   - Shaders written to HLSL's rules compile: a const local set from a
#     uniform (0042) and math on two vectors of different sizes
#     (glslang-0001). The second left the cat out of a Halloween wallpaper
#     and the first an ASCII-art wallpaper without its ASCII filter.
#   - A web preset's photo set through element.style shows (0029).
#
# 15: 28 patches on the pinned commit, applied in prepare(). Upstream main has
# not moved since b016d7d1 and its fixes sit in open pull requests, so 0001-0013
# and 0020 are those PRs as they stand (each names its PR; drop one when
# upstream merges it) and the rest are SynapseOS's own. Measured on the 139
# renderable wallpapers of a real Workshop library, one engine per output on a
# nested headless synui: 101 rendered correctly before, 130 after, and none
# got worse.
#
#   - Web wallpapers render (0 of 11 did). #687 gets CEF's windowless
#     rendering going; 0015 takes back the --in-process-gpu it adds, which on
#     NVIDIA killed 10 of the 11; 0018 implements the Wallpaper Engine web API
#     the pages are written against (their properties, the audio spectrum),
#     without which they sit black on their loading screen; 0017 lets the
#     wallpaper's files be found under a folder with a space in its name and
#     lets a <video> play. The web presets render with them (0 of 14 did), and
#     with 0024 a preset's own image or video shows: the page may load the
#     local files its properties name, and nothing else.
#   - Scene property scripts run (#698). Upstream broke them in June: no
#     update() has run since, so 1693 scripts across 29 wallpapers sat at their
#     static values, and createScriptProperties(), which 716 of them call,
#     returned nothing. Text layer scripts can import the built-in modules
#     (0021) and reach the scene through thisScene (0026), and
#     engine.registerAudioBuffers exists (0027): 168 scripts across 15
#     wallpapers read the audio spectrum through it and threw without it.
#   - Text with a space in it shows (0025). It was read as a vector, so 46
#     text layers across 13 wallpapers drew nothing.
#   - JSON with trailing commas and comments loads (0019). Wallpaper Engine
#     writes both, down to its own stock fluid simulation effect.
#   - A scene with an "auto" projection fills the screen instead of one
#     quadrant of it (0022). Integer colors read the way Wallpaper Engine
#     writes them (0014), and a text box nobody filled in no longer aborts the
#     wallpaper (0016).
#   - Leaks and crashes: a GL fence per video frame (#696), a PulseAudio
#     capture stream per device event (#694), album art (#644, #700), null
#     PulseAudio operations (#655), malformed binary assets (#631), and a
#     page, a text layer script that fails every frame, or a script probing a
#     layer for what it lacks no longer floods the log (0023, 0026, 0028).
#
# 14: a REBUILD and nothing else. ffmpeg 8 -> 9 moved libavcodec from .so.62 to
# .so.63 (and libavformat, libavutil, libswresample with it), and this package
# links all four directly, so the engine stopped starting the moment ffmpeg
# upgraded:
#
#   linux-wallpaperengine: error while loading shared libraries:
#   libavcodec.so.62: cannot open shared object file
#
# ⚠ AND IT FAILED WHERE NOBODY LOOKS. synui-wpengine redirects the engine into
# $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/synui-wpengine.<output>.log and launches it detached, so a
# wallpaper that never starts is indistinguishable from one that started and
# drew nothing: the picker reports success, the screen keeps synui's own
# wallpaper, and the reason is one line in a file in /run that is gone at the
# next reboot. The state file was then cleared by picking a static wallpaper
# instead, which is what a person does next — so the live wallpapers simply
# stopped, silently, with nothing left on disk to say why.
#
# `depends` names ffmpeg unversioned, which is correct — this is a soname
# coupling, not a version one, and the answer to a soname bump is a rebuild.
# Expect to bump this line again the next time ffmpeg rolls a major.
pkgrel=22
pkgdesc="Wallpaper Engine backgrounds on Wayland — layer-shell renderer for Steam Workshop wallpapers"
arch=('x86_64')
url="https://github.com/Almamu/linux-wallpaperengine"
license=('GPL-3.0-or-later')
# The renderer links these directly; mpv/ffmpeg drive video wallpapers, fftw
# and libpulse the audio-reactive ones, glfw/glew the GL context.
#
# freeglut, lz4, freetype2 and zlib are every remaining `find_package(...
# REQUIRED)` in upstream's CMakeLists that this list did not already name.
# They were missing because this package only ever built on a machine that
# happened to have them: makepkg runs with -fd here, so nothing checks depends
# and CMake just links the host's copy. The tell was freeglut on a freshly
# installed box —
#
#     Could NOT find GLUT (missing: GLUT_glut_LIBRARY GLUT_INCLUDE_DIR)
#
# — where the other three survived only because something else had pulled them
# in. Declared together because they fail as a class, not one at a time.
depends=('glew' 'sdl2-compat' 'ffmpeg' 'glfw' 'mpv' 'fftw' 'libpulse' 'libxrandr'
         'libx11' 'nss' 'nspr' 'alsa-lib' 'dbus' 'gtk3'
         'freeglut' 'lz4' 'freetype2' 'zlib')
makedepends=('git' 'cmake' 'glm' 'python')
# !debug, and it is not a preference: makepkg.conf carries `debug` globally, and
# splitting debug symbols out of CEF means objcopy walking a 1.3 GB libcef.so —
# it ran for over ten minutes here without finishing and yields a debug package
# nobody will ever load. Plain strip takes the same library to ~219 MB in about
# a minute, which is the only part worth having.
options=('!debug' 'strip')
optdepends=('steam: to subscribe to Workshop wallpapers'
            'synui: the compositor synui-wpengine drives')
# The engine reads Wallpaper Engine's own assets/ tree at runtime; it is not
# redistributable, so it stays where Steam put it (see synui-wpengine).

